      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Transform to a Mel spectrogram\n- Spectrograms can be changed to match how humans perceive audio.\n- Human ears can much more easily hear changes in low frequencies (e.g. 100-200 hz),\nthan difference between high frequencies (e.g. 10.000-10.100 hz). Linear scale doesn't\nfit how humans work!\n- Mel Spectrogram is that same format as spectrogram, the y-axis just follows the Mel\nscale: Bands of energy at the bottom will look taller and more spread out, while the\npatterns at the top will look compressed",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Transform to a Mel spectrogram\n- Spectrograms can be changed to match how humans perceive audio.\n- Human ears can much more easily hear changes in low frequencies (e.g. 100-200 hz), than difference between high frequencies (e.g. 10.000-10.100 hz). Linear scale doesn't fit how humans work!\n- Mel Spectrogram is that same format as spectrogram, the y-axis just follows the Mel scale: Bands of energy at the bottom will look taller and more spread out, while the patterns at the top will look compressed",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "_7yQHEuWVMgx7vL1iZr08",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": 4821.184183657793,
      "y": 1286.3989144668237,
      "width": 660.7370753421962,
      "height": 135,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
