      "locked": false,
      "text": "Shortcuts\n\n- Vanishing gradient problem\nIn deep networks, gradients can get smaller and smaller, so earlier layers\nstop learning\n\nShortcuts allow gradients to flow backward more directly\n\nHow it works\nThe input of a block is added to the output of that same block's\nconvolutional layers.\n\nWith shortcut: \nfinal_output = F(x) + x <=> F(x) = final_output - x\n\nWithout shortcut: \nfinal_output = F(x) <=> F(x) = final_output - x\n\n\n\nWithout a shortcut: A normal layer has to learn the entire final_output\nfrom scratch\n\nWith a shortcut: The layer only has to learn the change needed. If no\nchange is needed (residual), the main path F(x) can just learn to output\nzero, and the final_output will be x, perfectly preserving the information\n\nDuring back propagation here: final_output = F(x) + x, the plus operator\nmakes the gradient take two paths simultaneously, F(x) and x.\n\nThe total gradient that continues flowing to the layers before this block\nis the sum of the gradient from path 1 and the gradient from path 2. The\nshortcut's job is to make sure that even if the gradient in path 1 becomes\nvery weak, the strong, clean gradient from path 2 ensures a healthy signal\ncontinues to flow, preventing the training process from stalling.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Shortcuts\n\n- Vanishing gradient problem\nIn deep networks, gradients can get smaller and smaller, so earlier layers stop learning\n\nShortcuts allow gradients to flow backward more directly\n\nHow it works\nThe input of a block is added to the output of that same block's convolutional layers.\n\nWith shortcut: \nfinal_output = F(x) + x <=> F(x) = final_output - x\n\nWithout shortcut: \nfinal_output = F(x) <=> F(x) = final_output - x\n\n\n\nWithout a shortcut: A normal layer has to learn the entire final_output from scratch\n\nWith a shortcut: The layer only has to learn the change needed. If no change is needed (residual), the main path F(x) can just learn to output zero, and the final_output will be x, perfectly preserving the information\n\nDuring back propagation here: final_output = F(x) + x, the plus operator makes the gradient take two paths simultaneously, F(x) and x.\n\nThe total gradient that continues flowing to the layers before this block is the sum of the gradient from path 1 and the gradient from path 2. The shortcut's job is to make sure that even if the gradient in path 1 becomes very weak, the strong, clean gradient from path 2 ensures a healthy signal continues to flow, preventing the training process from stalling.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
